commit.message:
fix(perplexity): honor toolChoice none for all Agent API tools (#24413)

## Background

The Perplexity Agent API forwarded native MCP/connector configurations
and function tools when `toolChoice` was `none`, while only emitting an
unsupported-option warning. Presets can also enable tools without an
explicit tool list. This remains reproducible on main; the active v6
release branch already contains the equivalent guard.

## Summary

- Omit function and native tools, including MCP authorization and
headers, when tool choice is `none`.
- Override the native call budget with `max_tool_calls: 0` to disable
preset tools, even when provider options specify a positive limit.
- Treat `none` as supported and preserve automatic tool selection.
- Add generate/stream regression coverage for preset and direct model
IDs, update provider documentation, and add a patch changeset.

Validation: all 168 Perplexity tests pass in Node and all 168 pass in
Edge. Targeted lint/format checks and `git diff --check` pass. The
focused Perplexity type check and full workspace `pnpm type-check:full`
pass. The full check requires `pnpm --dir examples/nuxt-openai exec nuxt
prepare` to generate the example's local types after installing with
`--ignore-scripts`.

## End-to-End Verification

Built this checkout's local provider and dependencies before each run.
Exercised the exported `createPerplexity` provider through `doGenerate`
and `doStream`, using an actual local HTTP server that records the
serialized request and returns a deterministic Agent API response. The
temporary reproduction stays outside the checkout and contains no
assertions or test runner.

Prerequisites: supported Node.js, pnpm 12+, and installed workspace
dependencies (`pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --ignore-scripts`). No
provider credentials are needed for this local reproduction; every
credential below is a dummy value.

Run these commands from the checkout root before **each** run (before
the fix, and again after applying the fix):

```sh
pnpm exec turbo build --filter=@ai-sdk/perplexity... --cache=local:rw
cd examples/ai-functions
node --input-type=module < /tmp/ai-perplexity-tool-choice-none.mjs
```

`examples/ai-functions` resolves `@ai-sdk/perplexity` through its
workspace link to this checkout's `packages/perplexity/dist/index.js`,
rather than a registry package. Save the following complete script as
`/tmp/ai-perplexity-tool-choice-none.mjs`:

```js
import { createServer } from 'node:http';
import { createPerplexity } from '@ai-sdk/perplexity';

const server = createServer(async (request, response) => {
  let raw = '';
  for await (const chunk of request) raw += chunk;
  const body = JSON.parse(raw);
  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    stream: body.stream ?? false,
    toolTypes: body.tools?.map(tool => tool.type) ?? [],
    max_tool_calls: body.max_tool_calls ?? null,
    connectorCredentialsForwarded: raw.includes('dummy-connector-credential'),
  }));
  const result = {
    id: 'response-local', status: 'completed', created_at: 0,
    model: 'openai/gpt-5.1', object: 'response',
    output: [{ type: 'message', role: 'assistant', content: [{ type: 'output_text', text: 'Hello.' }] }],
    usage: { input_tokens: 1, output_tokens: 1, total_tokens: 2 },
  };
  if (body.stream) {
    response.setHeader('content-type', 'text/event-stream');
    response.end(`data: ${JSON.stringify({ type: 'response.completed', response: result })}\n\ndata: [DONE]\n\n`);
  } else {
    response.setHeader('content-type', 'application/json');
    response.end(JSON.stringify(result));
  }
});
await new Promise(resolve => server.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', resolve));
try {
  const perplexity = createPerplexity({
    baseURL: `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}`,
    apiKey: 'dummy-api-key',
  });
  for (const modelId of ['low', 'openai/gpt-5.1']) {
    const model = perplexity(modelId);
    const options = {
      prompt: [{ role: 'user', content: [{ type: 'text', text: 'Say hello.' }] }],
      toolChoice: { type: 'none' },
      tools: [{ type: 'function', name: 'get_weather', inputSchema: { type: 'object', properties: {} } }],
      providerOptions: { perplexity: {
        max_tool_calls: 5,
        tools: [
          { type: 'mcp', server_label: 'local', server_url: 'https://example.invalid/mcp', authorization: 'dummy-connector-credential' },
          { type: 'connector', id: 'dummy-connector', server_label: 'local' },
          { type: 'web_search' },
        ],
      } },
    };
    console.log(modelId);
    const generated = await model.doGenerate(options);
    console.log('warnings:', generated.warnings);
    const streamed = await model.doStream(options);
    for await (const part of streamed.stream) {
      if (part.type === 'error') throw part.error;
    }
  }
} finally {
  await new Promise(resolve => server.close(resolve));
}
```

For both `low` and `openai/gpt-5.1`, in both generation and streaming:

| Observed request | Before | After |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Tools | MCP, connector, web search, function | Omitted |
| `max_tool_calls` | 5 | 0 |
| Dummy connector credential forwarded | true | false |
| Generate warnings | Unsupported `toolChoice` | None |

Live Perplexity/MCP execution was not tested because
`PERPLEXITY_API_KEY` is unavailable in this session. The local
reproduction verifies request serialization and response handling; it
does not independently validate remote enforcement of Perplexity's
documented `max_tool_calls: 0` behavior. No remote connector was
contacted.

## Checklist

- [x] All commits are signed (PRs with unsigned commits cannot be
merged)
- [x] Tests have been added / updated (for bug fixes / features)
- [x] Documentation has been added / updated (for bug fixes / features)
- [x] A _patch_ changeset for relevant packages has been added (for bug
fixes / features - run `pnpm changeset` in the project root)
- [x] I have reviewed this pull request (self-review)

## Claude Code Review

Claude Code reviewed commit `f5906ee98b056d6ba03641dad45fd974abaf7332`
using model `claude-fable-5-1` and found **no actionable defects**. Two
minor regression coverage gaps (provider-defined tools with `none`, and
prior tool-call/result history) are addressed separately in stacked PR
#24414. The reviewer also noted that live preset enforcement,
skill/continuation behavior, and live acceptance of conversation history
require provider credentials and remain unverified.

