commit.message:
fix(examples): avoid logging MCP elicitation user information (#24691)

## Background

The MCP elicitation registration example requested a password and logged
the full submitted response, exposing credentials and email addresses to
server-log readers. The same flow also logged the accepted registration
payload and tool results containing the email.

The report identified `gr2m/backport-24573-24568-release-v5.0`; this PR
fixes current `main`, where the Next.js example lives under
`examples/ai-e2e-next/app/api/chat/mcp-elicitation`.

## Summary

- Remove form-content and raw parsing-error logging from the response
handler while retaining operational metadata.
- Remove accepted registration payload and tool-result logs that also
expose user information.
- Remove the password field from the registration schema, required
fields, type and README. Document that form elicitation must not request
credentials.
- Cover accept/decline/cancel resolution, unknown IDs and parsing errors
with regression tests, run through a dedicated package script and CI.

Changes are limited to examples and wiring their regression tests into
CI; no published-package changeset is needed.

## End-to-End Verification

The same local reproduction posts synthetic form data to the real
Next.js response endpoint and calls the real MCP registration tool over
SSE using rebuilt local workspace packages. It compares server logs and
the elicitation schema before and after the fix.

<details>
<summary>Reproduction code, setup, and before/after results</summary>

Prerequisites: Node.js 26.13.0 (or a supported Node version), pnpm
12.14.0, workspace dependencies, and available local ports 8085 and
3006. No provider API key is required. All submitted credentials and
email addresses are synthetic fixtures. Save this single script outside
the checkout as `/tmp/ai-sdk-mcp-elicitation-fix/reproduce.mjs`:

```js
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process';
import { createRequire } from 'node:module';
import { once } from 'node:events';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';
import { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';

const root = process.cwd();
const require = createRequire(resolve(root, 'examples/mcp/package.json'));
const mcpPath = require.resolve('@ai-sdk/mcp');
const { createMCPClient, ElicitationRequestSchema } = await import(pathToFileURL(mcpPath));
const fixture = {
  username: 'example-user',
  email: 'elicitation-fixture@example.invalid',
  password: 'elicitation-fixture-password',
  newsletter: false,
};
const output = { next: '', mcp: '' };
const children = [];

function start(name, args, cwd, readyText) {
  const child = spawn(process.execPath, args, { cwd, env: { ...process.env, NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED: '1' } });
  children.push(child);
  const ready = new Promise((resolveReady, rejectReady) => {
    const timeout = setTimeout(() => rejectReady(new Error(`${name} did not start within 60s`)), 60_000);
    const capture = chunk => {
      output[name] += chunk.toString();
      if (output[name].includes(readyText)) {
        clearTimeout(timeout);
        resolveReady();
      }
    };
    child.stdout.on('data', capture);
    child.stderr.on('data', capture);
    child.once('exit', code => {
      clearTimeout(timeout);
      rejectReady(new Error(`${name} exited with ${code}`));
    });
  });
  return ready;
}

let client;
try {
  console.log('Local MCP package:', mcpPath);
  await Promise.all([
    start('mcp', ['--import', require.resolve('tsx'), 'src/elicitation-ui/server.ts'], resolve(root, 'examples/mcp'), 'MCP elicitation UI server listening'),
    start('next', [resolve(root, 'examples/ai-e2e-next/node_modules/next/dist/bin/next'), 'dev', '--hostname', '127.0.0.1', '--port', '3006'], resolve(root, 'examples/ai-e2e-next'), 'Ready in'),
  ]);
  const response = await fetch('http://127.0.0.1:3006/api/chat/mcp-elicitation/respond', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ id: 'privacy-fixture', action: 'accept', content: fixture }),
  });
  console.log('Response endpoint:', response.status, await response.text());
  const malformed = await fetch('http://127.0.0.1:3006/api/chat/mcp-elicitation/respond', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: `${fixture.password} invalid JSON`,
  });
  console.log('Malformed response:', malformed.status, await malformed.text());
  client = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'sse', url: 'http://127.0.0.1:8085/sse' }, capabilities: { elicitation: {} } });
  client.onElicitationRequest(ElicitationRequestSchema, async request => {
    const schema = request.params.requestedSchema;
    console.log('Registration schema fields:', Object.keys(schema.properties).join(', '));
    console.log('Registration required fields:', schema.required.join(', '));
    const content = Object.fromEntries(Object.keys(schema.properties).map(key => [key, fixture[key]]));
    return { action: 'accept', content };
  });
  const tools = await client.tools();
  const result = await tools.register_user.execute({}, { toolCallId: 'registration-fixture', messages: [] });
  console.log('Registration tool completed:', result.content?.[0]?.text?.startsWith('Registration successful!'));
  await new Promise(resolveWait => setTimeout(resolveWait, 200));
  console.log('Endpoint log contains submitted password:', output.next.includes(fixture.password));
  console.log('Endpoint log contains submitted email:', output.next.includes(fixture.email));
  console.log('MCP server log contains submitted email:', output.mcp.includes(fixture.email));
} finally {
  await client?.close();
  await Promise.all(children.map(async child => {
    if (child.exitCode !== null) return;
    const exited = once(child, 'exit');
    child.kill('SIGTERM');
    await exited;
  }));
}
```

Run from the repository root before changing the example and again after
the fix:

```sh
pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
pnpm exec turbo build --filter=@ai-sdk/mcp... --filter=ai... --filter=@ai-sdk/otel...
node /tmp/ai-sdk-mcp-elicitation-fix/reproduce.mjs
```

The before run used `a6860a687011dbf025388ee419db730e05fb9ce0`; the
after run used the same checkout with the changes committed as
`76e0a5dc286c9116fafad864f25fad3261713826`. Both rebuilt the workspace
packages first. The later review-fix commit
`a29ac0140e635901bdd3bfd534642179a59680db` only wires the tests into a
package script and CI; it does not change application runtime code. The
printed MCP resolution is the checkout's `packages/mcp/dist/index.js`,
reached through the example's workspace symlink, rather than a registry
installation. The Next.js app and MCP server run from that same
checkout.

Before:

```text
Local MCP package: /Users/gr2m/.codex/worktrees/6571/ai/packages/mcp/dist/index.js
Response endpoint: 404 {"error":"Elicitation request not found or already resolved"}
Malformed response: 500 {"error":"Internal server error"}
Registration schema fields: username, email, password, newsletter
Registration required fields: username, email, password
Registration tool completed: true
Endpoint log contains submitted password: true
Endpoint log contains submitted email: true
MCP server log contains submitted email: true
```

After:

```text
Local MCP package: /Users/gr2m/.codex/worktrees/6571/ai/packages/mcp/dist/index.js
Response endpoint: 404 {"error":"Elicitation request not found or already resolved"}
Malformed response: 500 {"error":"Internal server error"}
Registration schema fields: username, email, newsletter
Registration required fields: username, email
Registration tool completed: true
Endpoint log contains submitted password: false
Endpoint log contains submitted email: false
MCP server log contains submitted email: false
```

The unknown-ID request intentionally returns 404: the original handler
leaked content before looking up the pending request. The malformed body
intentionally returns 500. The real MCP registration completes in both
runs, with the password absent from the final schema. This exercises the
Next.js HTTP handler and MCP SSE/tool flow directly; the LLM-driven
browser chat and its successful pending-response HTTP path were not
exercised. The route regression tests separately verify successful
resolution against the real pending store for all three actions.

</details>

## Validation and Review

- Regression tests: 5 passed. Before the fix, 3 failed and 2 passed.
- `pnpm --filter=@example/next-openai test:mcp-elicitation` (also run by
CI)
- `pnpm check`: passed (formatting and linting).
- `pnpm discover-guidelines --modified` and `pnpm run-verifiers
--modified`: no applicable guidelines/verifiers.
- `pnpm type-check:full`: passed.
- Claude Code Opus 5.5 (`claude-opus-5-5`): final full-diff review found
no remaining actionable issues. The initial missing test/CI entry point
finding was resolved in separate signed commit
`a29ac0140e635901bdd3bfd534642179a59680db` and confirmed on re-review.
- CI: all 53 checks passed for
`a29ac0140e635901bdd3bfd534642179a59680db`, including the new privacy
regression step and Vercel Agent review (zero suggestions). Backport
automation is inapplicable to this unmerged PR without a backport
request.

## Checklist

- [x] All commits are signed (PRs with unsigned commits cannot be
merged)
- [x] Tests have been added / updated (for bug fixes / features)
- [x] Documentation has been added / updated (for bug fixes / features)
- [ ] A _patch_ changeset for relevant packages has been added (for bug
fixes / features - run `pnpm changeset` in the project root)
- [x] I have reviewed this pull request (self-review)

